    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2507.01997v2-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Recent research has demonstrated the effectiveness of Artificial Intelligence (AI), and more specifically, Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Models</span> (<span class="search-hit mathjax">LLMs</span>), in supporting network configuration synthesis and automating network diagnosis tasks, among others. In this preliminary work, we restrict our f&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2507.01997v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2507.01997v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2507.01997v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Recent research has demonstrated the effectiveness of Artificial Intelligence (AI), and more specifically, Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Models</span> (<span class="search-hit mathjax">LLMs</span>), in supporting network configuration synthesis and automating network diagnosis tasks, among others. In this preliminary work, we restrict our focus to the application of AI agents to network troubleshooting and elaborate on the need for a standardized, reproducible, and open benchmarking platform, where to build and evaluate AI agents with low operational effort.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2507.01997v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2507.01997v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 4 July, 2025; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 1 July, 2025;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> July 2025.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">Accepted at ACM SIGCOMM 1st Workshop on Next-Generation Network Observability (NGNO), 2025</span>
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

